<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Mail poll — reads matthew@mrecai.com and files what is in it.

  Install:
    cp ops/com.lms.mail.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.lms.mail.plist

  Run it once by hand before trusting the schedule:
    launchctl kickstart -p gui/$(id -u)/com.lms.mail

  Stop:
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.lms.mail

  READ-ONLY, AND NOT BY PROMISE
  The OAuth grant this uses is gmail.readonly (D-039). It cannot delete a
  message, cannot mark one read, and cannot write a label — Google refuses,
  rather than this software declining. Scheduling it therefore adds no risk
  that running it by hand did not already carry.

  A LaunchAgent, not a LaunchDaemon, and that is required: the refresh token
  lives in the user's Keychain, which a system daemon cannot read. Same reason
  as the backup job.

  A TWO-DAY WINDOW, NOT ONE. It deliberately overlaps itself.

  (Written as "two-day" rather than with the flag spelled out because an XML
  comment may not contain a double hyphen. launchd refuses to load a plist
  whose comments do, and the failure is a job that silently never runs.)

  Re-reading yesterday is free: identity is the sha256 of the rendered message,
  which does not change between polls, so the second sighting files nothing and
  prints DUPLICATE. The overlap is what makes a missed run harmless — if the
  Mac is asleep at 07:00 the next run still catches the mail, and nothing needs
  a repair script. A stored cursor would have been cheaper and would lose mail
  permanently the first time a run died between reading and committing.

  StartCalendarInterval, not StartInterval: if the machine is off at the
  appointed hour launchd runs the job at the next opportunity, where an
  interval timer would simply skip the day. C8 — who unlocks the machine after
  a power cut — is still unanswered, so missed windows are a live scenario.

  07:00 and 19:00, and the morning run lands AFTER the 06:30 brief rather than
  before it. That is a deliberate compromise and worth naming.

  Polling at 06:00 would put a network call and up to twenty inference passes
  directly in front of the one thing that has to arrive on time, and a slow
  mailbox would delay it. So mail filed at 07:00 appears in the NEXT morning's
  brief — at most a day late. Matthew asked for a daily rhythm, not an alerting
  system, and the evening run means most of a day's mail is already filed before
  the brief is built. If this turns out to be the wrong trade, the fix is to
  move the brief later, not to move the poll earlier.

  NO CREDENTIAL APPEARS HERE. A plist is world-readable and lands in git. The
  refresh token is read from the Keychain at run time (D-016, D-039).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.lms.mail</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Users/mleca/lms-repo/lms/.venv/bin/python</string>
        <string>/Users/mleca/lms-repo/lms/ops/poll_mail.py</string>
        <string>matthew@mrecai.com</string>
        <string>--days</string>
        <string>2</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/mleca/lms-repo/lms</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- NO LMS_* PATHS HERE. poll_mail.py reads ops/lms.env itself.
             See D-035, and com.lms.watchfolder.plist for the second time it
             happened. tests/test_plists.py enforces this. -->
        <key>TZ</key>
        <string>America/New_York</string>
        <key>PYTHONUNBUFFERED</key>
        <string>1</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>Hour</key>
            <integer>7</integer>
            <key>Minute</key>
            <integer>0</integer>
        </dict>
        <dict>
            <key>Hour</key>
            <integer>19</integer>
            <key>Minute</key>
            <integer>0</integer>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!-- One-shot. It reads the window and exits; KeepAlive would restart it
         in a loop and re-poll the mailbox continuously. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/mleca/LMS/logs/mail.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/mleca/LMS/logs/mail.err</string>

    <!-- Below normal. OCR and inference must never make the machine feel slow
         while Matthew is using it. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
